  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 104 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nGOES Water Vapor satellite trends place an upper level low over\nthe Tennessee Valley this afternoon. Regional WSR-88D radar\ntrends continue to show light precipitation occurring across\nareas mostly north of I-40. Temperatures early this afternoon\nrange from the lower to middle 30s across much of West Tennessee\nand upper 30s to lower 40s elsewhere.\n\nLatest ACRS data shows the atmospheric temperature profile is\nbelow zero except for the lowest 1200 ft. Thus, most of the\nprecipitation fell as rain this morning, except across the\nnorthern third of the Mid-South where temperatures were\nsufficiently cool for snowflakes to mix in with the rain and\nreach the ground. Latest short-term model trends suggest this\nactivity will continue to linger into the remainder of the\nafternoon and decrease into this evening as the upper-level low\npushes east away from the Lower Mississippi Valley. Any\nadditional light precipitation may produce up to an additional\ninch of snow accumulation across the advisory area. The Winter\nWeather Advisory will likely be allowed to expire at 4 PM unless\ntrends suggest otherwise.\n\nShort-wave upper-level ridging will build into the Mid-South for\nFriday and Saturday behind this upper-level low with drier and\nwarmer conditions expected for Friday and Saturday. Highs on\nFriday will range from the middle to upper 40s near the Tennessee\nRiver, and lower to middle 50s elsewhere, and upper 50s to lower\n60s on Saturday. Overnight lows will fall back into the upper 20s\nto lower 30s east of the Mississippi River, and 30s west.\n\nToday's deterministic and ensemble model solutions continue to\nindicate a northern stream mid-level trough moving across the\nMississippi Valley late this weekend. This will bring a return of\nrain showers to the area with confidence remaining very low for\nthunderstorms as both the NBM and LREF probabilities for surface\nor elevated instability remain low, generally 100 J/kg or less.\nSlightly above normal temperatures and drier weather are expected\nfor most of next week with upper-level ridging building across\nthe region as an upper-level low drops into the western U.S. This\nnext upper-level low may bring a return of rain chances back to\nthe area towards the end of next week.",
